// Issue #208 regression: error formatting used to crash on a malformed type.
//
// ADT variant fields are bare (see docs/ta-language-spec.md: `Some(value)`,
// `Ping(Pid)`); a `field : type` annotation inside a variant is not part of
// the grammar. The parser's variant-arg path (expression call args) silently
// mis-parses `num : int` and hands typecheck a nil field, from which
// resolve_field_type manufactures the malformed type ('app nil). Formatting
// that type in an error message then crashed the whole compiler
// ("str.sym_to_str: expected symbol, got tag=0xfff2" = TAG_NIL), hiding the
// real type errors. Fix: fmt_type_name renders a non-symbol head as the raw
// marker <?type> so the message always prints.
//
// Calling R(x, 1) then fails to unify int against the malformed field type;
// the compiler must reject the file with that readable [E0001] line — never
// a crash (pre-fix this file died in str.sym_to_str mid-report).
import result

pub type Rational { R(num : int, den : int) }

pub fn f(x : int) -> Result(Rational, string) { Ok(R(x, 1)) }

fn main() -> int { 0 }

// expect: cannot unify int with <?type>